    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full suite is green \u2014 **279 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` pipeline now computes a namesake count at creation time and it flows through to the owner response (and subsequent `GET`s, since it's persisted like the other creation-time attributes).\n\n1. **New function** `AssignOwnerNamesakeCount.java` \u2014 counts existing owners sharing the new owner's last name (the owner isn't saved yet, so it's never counted among its own namesakes: first with a surname \u2192 0, second \u2192 1).\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new step into the pipeline between `assignMembershipTier` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance as its neighbours.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter, matching the existing membership-field pattern.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` int32 property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name automatically (no mapper change needed).\n\n5. **Schema SQL** \u2014 added the `namesake_count INTEGER` column to all four DB schemas (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres). This is required because hsqldb (the test DB) runs with `ddl-auto=none` and relies on the explicit schema.\n\nI followed the codebase convention (`AssignOwnerCustomerCode`'s `city.equals(...)`) and matched last names by exact equality. The existing `Cp16Tests` acceptance test \u2014 which creates two owners with the same surname and reads `namesakeCount` back via `GET` \u2014 passes (asserting 0 then 1).",
